  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nDevelop a hybrid algorithm combining gradient descent with intelligent global exploration. The core idea is to leverage the efficiency of gradient-based optimization for refining candidate sequences while using a carefully designed random walk strategy to escape local minima. The algorithm will explore multiple sequence lengths, but we'll prioritize fine-tuning the current best candidate with a more sophisticated gradient method. This approach is different from the previous multi-start strategy as it uses a single, adaptive search direction that updates based on the current best solution. It attempts to balance exploration (dodging local minima) and exploitation (refining promising candidates) in a more continuous manner.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nWe represent the step function as a sequence/a of coefficients a_0, a_1, ..., a_{n-1}. The evaluation function is:\nf(a) = (2*n*max(convolve(a, a, mode='full'))) / (sum(a)^2)\nwhere convolve is linear convolution.\nThe core optimization view is maximizing or minimizing the peak of the self-convolution, but with a finite sum.\n\nThe algorithm involves:\n- Balanced initialization with fixed length n=256\n- A core iterative procedure that maintains a \"beam\" of the best candidates and explore good mutation directions\n- Convexity is preserved by keeping non-negative coefficients\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nThe propose_candidate function will:\n- Initialize a sequence of length 256 using a combination of Saab_Parand and optimization-normalized versions (avoiding extremes)\n- Maintain an active candidate sequence\n- Use gradient information to guide the search:\n  * Compute gradient of the evaluation function at the current candidate\n  * Select the largest peak contributor and perturb it slightly while maintaining non-negativity\n  * Gradually adjust the contribution by using a metadata-normalization technique\n- Perform targeted mutations to the sequence (both increasing and decreasing specific coefficients) to explore the solution space\n- Repeat the process for up to 2400 seconds, with periodic evaluations\n- If a sufficiently low score is found, perform a final validation\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\nUse a dual-pronged strategy:\nConfiguration 1: Focus on n=256, using gradient-based optimization to refine around the initial values\nConfiguration 2: Smart random walks: 80% of the time, perform small gradient-based mutations to promising areas, 20% for exploration use large random steps (with safeguards against negative/NaN/huge values) and random sequence maskings\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\nEach evaluation: FFT-based convolution (O(n^2) operation with optimizations)\nWe'll run the search for 1200 seconds (half of the total time) and ensure the gradient descent steps are time-controlled. The algorithm includes intelligent time management:\n- 80% of the time budget for gradient descent-based refinement\n- 20% for entering new areas of the search domain\nIterative termination conditions are in place, with the best candidate saved after each epoch. The code handles NaN/inf/invalid inputs by direct evaluation, not surrogates.\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\nAt the end of the search time, the best candidate is re-evaluated to compare scores. The algorithm ensures all candidates are positive and bounded (0 to 1000). Final output is a list of 256 positive numbers that theoretically minimizes the evaluation function.",
